Peskov further argued that European governments are paying for their policies in “hard figures—macroeconomic indicators and energy prices,” claiming that while BRICS nations may hold nuanced views, they broadly align with Russia’s vision of the global order.

The escalating rhetoric coincides with renewed diplomatic positioning surrounding the upcoming G20 Leaders' Summit in Miami, scheduled for 14–15 December:

▪️ Summit attendance in question: Presidential foreign policy aide Yuri Ushakov told Izvestia that Vladimir Putin’s potential travel to Miami has “not yet been discussed” within the Kremlin, noting that preparations remain confined to expert-level document reviews. This follows Peskov’s earlier July statements that Russia would maintain “high-level, substantive” representation at the U.S.-hosted summit.

▪️ Zelenskyy’s conditional offer: Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy confirmed to Deutsche Welle that he is prepared to travel to Miami for a direct, face-to-face meeting with Putin, contingent on the Russian leader attending in person. According to the host venue's published roster, Ukraine does not currently appear on the list of invited non-G20 observer states.

▪️ Lavrov’s dismissal and combat continuity: Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov claimed to state media correspondent Pavel Zarubin that “an invitation has been sent to this person,” framing Moscow’s openness to engagement as a “gesture of goodwill.” Lavrov seized on Kyiv’s intact presidential decree barring direct negotiations with Putin, while emphasizing that military operations will continue unabated: “We remain open to negotiations, but the special military operation will not be suspended while talks take place. We have learned that lesson.”

▪️ The Moscow deflection: Peskov subsequently deflected the prospect of an encounter on U.S. soil, reiterating the Kremlin’s standard counter-condition: “If Zelenskyy is genuinely seeking a meeting with Putin, Putin has made it clear that he is welcome to fly to Moscow. That invitation remains open.”

The sparring over Miami reflects another round of performative posturing ahead of the winter diplomatic calendar. By conditioning talks on Zelenskyy travelling to Moscow or accepting unpaused frontline offensives, the Kremlin continues to treat diplomatic summitry as an instrument for projecting leverage rather than a genuine pathway toward de-escalation.

